==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|ruke}}, from {{der|en|non|-}}. Compare {{cog|is|hrúka}}, {{cog|sv|ruka}}.

====Pronunciation====
* {{IPA|en|/ɹʌk/}}
* {{audio|en|En-au-ruck.ogg|Audio (AU)}}
** {{a|Northern England}} {{IPA|en|/ɹʊk/}}
* {{homophone|en|rook|q1=most accents without foot-strut split}}
* {{rhymes|en|ʌk|s=1}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# A [[throng]] or [[crowd]] of people or things; a [[mass]], a [[pack]]. {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
#* {{quote-book|1=en|year=1873|author=w:Anthony Trollope|title=Phineas Redux|url=http://etext.virginia.edu/etcbin/ot2www-pubeng?specfile=%2Ftexts%2Fenglish%2Fmodeng%2Fpublicsearch%2Fmodengpub.o2w|section=Chapter 16|text=Dandolo was constantly in the ditch, sometimes lying with his side against the bank, and had now been so hustled and driven that, had he been on the other side, he would have had no breath left to carry his rider, even in the '''ruck''' of the hunt.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20140811201712/http://etext.virginia.edu/etcbin/ot2www-pubeng?specfile=%2Ftexts%2Fenglish%2Fmodeng%2Fpublicsearch%2Fmodengpub.o2w|archivedate=11 August 2014}}
# {{non-gloss definition|In [[Australian rules football]]}}
## A contest in games in which the ball is thrown or bounced in the air and two players from opposing teams attempt to give their team an advantage, typically by tapping the ball to a [[teammate]].
## A player who competes in said contests; a [[ruckman]] or [[ruckwoman]].
## {{lb|en|now|rare}} Either of a [[ruckman]] or a [[ruck rover]], but not a [[rover]].
## Any one of a [[ruckman]], a [[ruck rover]] or a [[rover]]; a [[follower]].
# {{lb|en|rugby union}} The situation formed when a player carrying the ball is brought to the [[ground]] and one or more members of each side are engaged above the [[ball]], trying to win [[possession]] of it; a [[loose scrum]]. {{defdate|from 20th c.}}
# The common mass of people or things; the ordinary ranks. {{defdate|from 19th c.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1911|author=w:Saki|chapter=Tobermory|title=The Chronicles of Clovis|text=‘Here and there among cats one comes across an outstanding superior intellect, just as one does among the '''ruck''' of human beings [...].’}}
# {{lb|en|colloquial}} An [[argument]] or [[fight]].
#* {{quote-web|en|url=https://www.theguardian.com/lifeandstyle/2023/jan/28/62-dating-green-flags-that-shout-this-ones-a-keeper|title=62 dating green flags that shout ‘this one’s a keeper’|date=2023-01-28|author=Justin Myers|work=The Guardian|passage=Your worth as a couple is not down to how passionate your '''rucks''' are—I said '''rucks'''—and how frantic the making-up sex is. }}

=====Usage notes=====
In the second Australian rules football sense, "ruck" is a gender-neutral term. "[[ruckman|Ruckman]]" is sometimes considered to refer only to men, but is often considered gender-neutral. "[[ruckwoman|Ruckwoman]]" only refers to women.

